      "page_hit": true,
      "answer": {
        "status": "answered",
        "answer": "Structural recursion on a finite list terminates because the function is defined with a base case that handles the empty list and a recursive case that consumes the tail of the list, producing a strictly shorter list on each call. This guarantees that after a finite number of steps the empty list is reached, at which point recursion stops.",
        "citations": [
          {
            "source_id": "ede32e8bfc162cb6b1f49972dd0b737f",
            "document_id": "sample-Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists",
            "filename": "Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists.pdf",
            "page": 1,
            "quote": "A recursive function calls itself on a smaller problem. A base case handles an input without further recursion; a recursive case reduces the input toward that base case. Without a reachable base case or a decreasing problem, evaluation may not terminate."
          },
          {
            "source_id": "ede32e8bfc162cb6b1f49972dd0b737f",
            "document_id": "sample-Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists",
            "filename": "Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists.pdf",
            "page": 1,
            "quote": "Each recursive call consumes the tail, a strictly shorter finite list."
