      "page_hit": true,
      "answer": {
        "status": "answered",
        "answer": "In Haskell, the colon operator (:) is a list constructor that prepends a single element to an existing list. It is a constant\u2011time operation because it only creates a new cons cell pointing to the original list. The append operator (++) concatenates two lists by traversing the entire left list; its cost is linear in the length of that left list. Thus, using (:) is cheaper than (++) when building a list element by element, whereas (++) is useful only when you need to join two already\u2011existing lists.\n",
        "citations": [
          {
            "source_id": "daf10f33b4bded3558f58eae2c2e5a69",
            "document_id": "sample-Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists",
            "filename": "Lecture-2-Recursion-and-Lists.pdf",
            "page": 2,
            "quote": "The colon constructor prepends one element to a list. The expression 1 : [2,3] is [1,2,3]. The append operator ++ joins two lists; [1,2] ++ [3] is [1,2,3]. Traversing the left list makes append linear in the length of that left list."
          }
        ]
      },
